# Story: As a security officer, I want repeated failed sign-ins to lock an account for a while, so that passwords cannot be guessed by trying many of them. After five failed sign-ins for one username within fifteen minutes, sign-in for that username is refused for five minutes with TOO_MANY_ATTEMPTS, even with the right password. Other accounts are not affected.

@draft
Feature: Account Lockout After Multiple Failed Sign-In Attempts

  Scenario Outline: Five failed sign-ins lock an account for five minutes
    Given the user has attempted to sign in with incorrect credentials four times within fifteen minutes
    When they attempt to sign in a fifth time with incorrect credentials
    Then the system refuses access and locks the account for five minutes
    And when they try to sign in again before the lockout period ends
    Then the system returns TOO_MANY_ATTEMPTS

    Examples:
      | username |

  Scenario Outline: Correct password does not unlock an account during lockout period
    Given the user has attempted to sign in with incorrect credentials five times within fifteen minutes
    When they attempt to sign in a sixth time with correct credentials while locked out
    Then the system refuses access and returns TOO_MANY_ATTEMPTS

    Examples:
      | username |

  Scenario Outline: Account unlocks after lockout period ends
    Given the user has attempted to sign in with incorrect credentials five times within fifteen minutes
    When they wait for the account lockout period to end
    And attempt to sign in again with correct credentials
    Then the system allows access

    Examples:
      | username |

  Scenario Outline: Other accounts are not affected by a locked account
    Given another user has attempted to sign in with incorrect credentials five times within fifteen minutes
    When they wait for their lockout period to end
    And attempt to sign in again with correct credentials
    Then the system allows access

    Examples:
      | username |
